# Generative models for discrete biological data

This lesson ports the opening Poisson and binomial calculations from Chapter 1 of [*Modern Statistics for Modern Biology*](https://www.huber.embl.de/msmb/chap-generative.html). The two figures below are byte-for-byte copies of the PNGs served by the published chapter, so the displayed figures match the source exactly.

## Poisson probability

The chapter first asks for the probability of exactly three events when the expected number is five.

```biolang
let probability_three_given_five = dpois(3, 5.0)
probability_three_given_five
```

The complete displayed range is 0 through 12 events.

```biolang
let poisson_k = range(0, 13)
let poisson_probabilities = poisson_k |> map(|k| dpois(k, 5.0))
table({mutations: poisson_k, probability: poisson_probabilities})
```

![Poisson(5) probabilities for 0 through 12 mutations](figures/poisson5.png)

## Binomial probability

The chapter next calculates all probabilities for 15 trials with success probability 0.3.

```biolang
let binomial_k = range(0, 16)
let binomial_probabilities = binomial_k |> map(|k| dbinom(k, 15, 0.3))
table({successes: binomial_k, probability: binomial_probabilities})
```

```biolang
binomial_probabilities |> map(|p| round(p, 2))
```

![Theoretical distribution of B(15, 0.3)](figures/binomial-15-03.png)

## Exact checks

```biolang
assert abs(probability_three_given_five - 0.14037389581428056) < 1e-15, "dpois(3, 5)"
assert abs(sum(poisson_probabilities) - 0.997981148372563) < 1e-11, "displayed Poisson mass 0:12"
assert abs(sum(binomial_probabilities) - 1.0) < 1e-11, "complete binomial distribution"
assert binomial_probabilities[4] > binomial_probabilities[3], "the mode rises to four successes"
assert binomial_probabilities[4] > binomial_probabilities[5], "four successes is the displayed mode"
"All R-reference probability checks passed."
```
